    articles: [
      { id: "a1", kick: "INVESTIGATION", sev: "CRITICAL", time: "07:58", read: 14, author: "Mara Okafor", role: "Chief Investigations Editor", date: "JUL 14, 2026", img: "photo: seized fake trading dashboard", rel: ["s1", "s8"],
        title: "Inside the $47M 'Pig-Butchering' Ring That Ran on Fake Exchange Apps",
        dek: "A six-month trail of shell domains, cloned trading dashboards and 3,800 victims — and the four red flags that were visible from day one.",
        body: [
          { k: "p", t: "For six months, the operators of \"NovaTrade Pro\" ran what looked like a boutique derivatives exchange: a polished dashboard, a 24/7 support desk, even a proof-of-reserves page. None of it was real. Our review of 3,800 victim statements, domain records and on-chain flows shows a single syndicate moving at least $47 million through 61 cloned exchange front-ends." },
          { k: "h", t: "The romance funnel" },
          { k: "p", t: "Victims never found NovaTrade through an ad. They were walked in — patiently — by \"mentors\" met on dating apps and language-exchange groups. The playbook is industrial: three weeks of small talk, a screenshot of a winning trade, then an invitation to \"practice\" with $200." },
          { k: "q", t: "They let me withdraw twice. That's what convinced me. The third deposit was my pension." },
          { k: "p", t: "Withdrawals worked until they didn't. Once an account crossed roughly $15,000, the dashboard invented a reason to freeze it: a \"withdrawal insurance fee\", a \"tax clearance\", an \"account upgrade\". Each fee was payable only by fresh deposit." },
          { k: "c", title: "Four red flags visible from day one", items: ["Domain registered 11 days before the first deposit — always check registration dates.", "\"Proof of reserves\" page had no verifiable on-chain addresses.", "Support pushed victims from email to Telegram within one message.", "Withdrawal fees payable only by new deposit. No legitimate venue does this."] },
          { k: "p", t: "The database entry for NovaTrade Pro and its 60 sibling domains is live, with wallet clusters and indicator hashes. If a \"mentor\" is coaching you into any platform on that list, stop. Ask the forum first — the community answers in minutes." }
        ] },
      { id: "a2", kick: "NEWS", sev: "CRITICAL", time: "09:41", read: 6, author: "Dev Patel", role: "Senior Reporter", date: "JUL 14, 2026", img: "photo: phone with fake wallet app", rel: ["s2"],
        title: "Fake 'Ledger Live' App Slips Past Play Store Review — 12,000 Downloads Before Takedown",
        dek: "The trojanized wallet asked users to 'restore' their 24 words on first launch. Google pulled it in 72 hours; the seeds were gone in minutes.",
        body: [
          { k: "p", t: "The listing looked immaculate: the right logo, a plausible changelog, 4.6 stars from farmed reviews. \"Ledger Live 3.0 — Official Update\" sat in search results for three days and was installed more than 12,000 times before Google removed it." },
          { k: "h", t: "Verify signatures, every time" },
          { k: "p", t: "The app itself did almost nothing — except at first launch, where a pixel-perfect \"restore your wallet\" screen collected 24-word phrases and posted them to a server in real time. Drains began within four minutes of entry, our on-chain review shows." },
          { k: "c", title: "Protect yourself", items: ["Download wallet apps only from links on the vendor's own domain.", "Check the developer name and release hash before installing.", "No app ever needs your full seed to 'sync' or 'verify'.", "Typed a seed into a screen? Assume it is burned. Move funds now."] }
        ] },
      { id: "a3", kick: "THREAT INTEL", sev: "HIGH", time: "10:24", read: 8, author: "Lena Vogt", role: "Threat Intel Lead", date: "JUL 14, 2026", img: "diagram: drainer kit rental flow", rel: ["s3"],
        title: "Drainer-as-a-Service Kit 'Inferno v4' Now Rents for $99 a Week",
        dek: "The kit behind most fake mints this quarter ships with cloned UIs, approval-swap scripts and a revenue dashboard for its 'affiliates'.",
        body: [
          { k: "p", t: "Wallet draining is no longer a skill — it's a subscription. Inferno v4, the kit our tracker links to over 400 live phishing deployments, now advertises weekly rentals with onboarding support and a 20% platform fee on stolen funds." },
          { k: "h", t: "Crime, subscription-priced" },
          { k: "p", t: "The kit's core trick is unchanged: get a signature that looks routine and swap in a max-allowance approval. What's new is scale — templates for 40 protocols, one-click clones of mint pages, and A/B testing on button copy. Treat every unexpected signature request as hostile, and read what you sign on the device screen." }
        ] },
      { id: "a4", kick: "NEWS", sev: "HIGH", time: "11:05", read: 5, author: "Dev Patel", role: "Senior Reporter", date: "JUL 14, 2026", img: "still: deepfake giveaway stream", rel: ["s6"],
        title: "Deepfake 'Saylor' Giveaway Streams Are Back — 240 Channels Pulled This Weekend",
        dek: "The 'send one, get two back' hustle now uses cloned voices and hijacked verified channels. Nobody is doubling your bitcoin.",
        body: [
          { k: "p", t: "The streams look live, the voice is close, and the QR code is always in the corner: send BTC to \"verify your address\" and receive double back. This weekend's wave used hijacked verified channels with six-figure subscriber counts, lending each stream borrowed credibility." },
          { k: "h", t: "The oldest math in the book" },
          { k: "p", t: "Giveaway scams survive because the promise is simple and the clock is fake — 'only 20 spots left' has been left for four years. The rule is shorter than the scam: nobody doubles your coins. Ever. Report streams to the database; takedowns are fastest in the first hour." }
        ] },
      { id: "a5", kick: "FIELD GUIDE", sev: "", time: "08:30", read: 7, author: "Lena Vogt", role: "Threat Intel Lead", date: "JUL 13, 2026", img: "diagram: address poisoning attack", rel: ["s7"],
        title: "Address Poisoning, Explained: Why You Should Never Copy From History",
        dek: "Attackers seed your history with lookalike addresses that match the first and last four characters. One careless paste is all it takes.",
        body: [
          { k: "p", t: "Your transaction history feels like a safe place to copy addresses from. That's exactly what address poisoning weaponizes: attackers grind vanity addresses that match the first and last four characters of your real counterparties, then send you $0 'dust' so the lookalike sits in your history, waiting." },
          { k: "h", t: "How the trap is set" },
          { k: "p", t: "Address generation is cheap. Matching eight characters costs an attacker seconds of compute; matching your habits costs them nothing, because most people verify addresses by ends alone. The middle is where the theft lives." },
          { k: "c", title: "The countermeasures", items: ["Verify the full address, character by character — or don't send.", "Use your wallet's address book for repeat destinations.", "Send a test amount on any first-time transfer.", "Treat $0 and dust transfers in your history as hostile."] },
          { k: "p", t: "Poisoned-pair reports are up 4× this quarter on TRON and EVM chains. If you've been dusted, report the lookalike to the database — clusters help everyone downstream." }
        ] },
      { id: "a6", kick: "FIELD GUIDE", sev: "", time: "12:12", read: 6, author: "Mara Okafor", role: "Chief Investigations Editor", date: "JUL 12, 2026", img: "photo: recovery scam dm thread", rel: ["s9"],
        title: "The 'Recovery Agent' Who Scams You Twice",
        dek: "After the rug comes the DM: 'We can trace your funds.' The second scam targets people at their most desperate — here's the script, line by line.",
        body: [
          { k: "p", t: "Hours after a scam collapses, its victims become a market. Lists of the defrauded are sold to \"recovery agents\" who slide into DMs with badges, case numbers and a promise: for an upfront fee, your coins come home. They never do." },
          { k: "h", t: "The script" },
          { k: "p", t: "It's always the same four beats: false authority ('blockchain forensics unit'), false urgency ('funds move to a mixer Friday'), a small fee ('court filing costs'), then a bigger one. Some agents are the original scammers, harvesting twice." },
          { k: "c", title: "The rules", items: ["No legitimate firm DMs victims first. None.", "Upfront 'gas' or 'filing' fees to release funds are always fake.", "Real tracing ends in court orders, not Telegram chats.", "Report recovery pitches — they cluster around fresh collapses."] }
        ] },
      { id: "a7", kick: "COMMUNITY WIN", sev: "", time: "13:40", read: 9, author: "Mara Okafor", role: "Chief Investigations Editor", date: "JUL 11, 2026", img: "photo: court filing stack", rel: ["s4"],
        title: "Court Freezes $9.8M Tied to 'BitVault Capital' After Community Dossier",
        dek: "A 214-page evidence pack assembled by forum members became Exhibit A. The freeze order cites wallet clusters first mapped in our threads.",
        body: [
          { k: "p", t: "On July 8, a district court froze $9.8 million connected to BitVault Capital, the self-described 'institutional BTC yield desk' that stopped honoring withdrawals in May. Paragraph 12 of the order cites a wallet-cluster analysis that will look familiar to readers: it was assembled in public, in this forum." },
          { k: "h", t: "How the dossier came together" },
          { k: "p", t: "Forty-one contributors, eight weeks, one shared methodology: every claim sourced, every address verified twice, every speculation labeled as such. The moderation team packaged 214 pages for investigators. This is what community verification is for." },
          { k: "q", t: "Forty strangers with spreadsheets beat a 'fund' with a Mayfair address." }
        ] },
      { id: "a8", kick: "FIELD GUIDE", sev: "", time: "06:15", read: 5, author: "Iris Chen", role: "Newsletter Editor", date: "JUL 10, 2026", img: "photo: hardware wallet on desk", rel: ["s3"],
        title: "Five Wallet-Hygiene Rules the Pros Actually Follow",
        dek: "No hardware fetishism, no paranoia theater — just the habits that consistently keep coins where they belong.",
        body: [
          { k: "p", t: "Ask ten security researchers how they hold coins and you'll get boring answers. That's the point. Everything on this list is free or nearly free, takes minutes, and would have prevented the majority of losses in our database." },
          { k: "c", title: "The five rules", items: ["One hot wallet for spending, one cold for savings. They never mix.", "Approvals are debts — revoke what you don't use, quarterly.", "Verify receive addresses on the device screen, not the browser.", "Your seed lives on steel, offline, in two places. It never travels.", "Rehearse recovery once a year, before you need it."] },
          { k: "p", t: "Print the list. Tape it somewhere embarrassing. Boring habits are the ones that survive contact with a good scam." }
        ] },
      { id: "a9", kick: "EXCHANGE WATCH", sev: "HIGH", time: "14:02", read: 4, author: "Lena Vogt", role: "Threat Intel Lead", date: "JUL 14, 2026", img: "chart: withdrawal complaint volume", rel: ["s8"],
